R8.64 established Path Dependency.

The next boundary emerges naturally:

> What if returning to an earlier state is technically possible, but the system no longer behaves the same way when it gets there?



That is the problem of hysteresis.

A system may occupy the same apparent state while carrying a different history, dependency structure, expectation field and authority distribution.

Therefore:

SAME STATE
≠
SAME CONSTITUTIONAL CONDITION


---

I. 🌱 CONSTITUTIONAL HYSTERESIS

R8.65 introduces:

CONSTITUTIONAL_HYSTERESIS

Definition:

> A condition in which the system's present behavior or constitutional configuration depends materially on the path through which that configuration was reached, such that returning to an earlier apparent state does not restore the earlier constitutional conditions.



The essential distinction:

STATE RETURN
   ≠
CONSTITUTIONAL RESTORATION


